WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.280
JOE: If change is inevitable then
individuals with autism can be helped

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.900
and guided through the process by
using strategies to prepare and

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.840
support them through those changes.

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:13.530
BETH: If a change is due to happen
at school, parents and carers

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:16.600
can be proactive in finding out
about what the changes will be.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.740
If there is to be a new teacher
or new classrooms, by finding

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:24.670
out what is involved in advance,
parents and carers can work out any

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.759
impact it might have on a child.

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:30.570
JOE: The day of the change can be marked
on a calendar and the child can be

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.800
encouraged to count down to that day.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.370
Describing the change clearly
gives the child time to process the

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.800
information along with visual supports.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.000
Visual supports like social stories
and photos can be used to show the

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.050
outcome of activities such as going
on holiday and all that goes with it.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.890
BETH: Showing a photo of a plane and a
hotel may make an individual nervous to go

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.999
on a plane or concerned about the hotel.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.750
But if the whole process is
documented from start to finish

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.740
and shown visually including lots
of photos of the destination, this

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.269
will help them to understand more.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.810
The whole process can then be reversed
to show the journey back and show that

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.470
they are going to be returning home
to somewhere and something familiar.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.770
Visual timetables can also help to
make the processes more straightforward

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.520
and prepare them for the trip.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.830
JOE: Visual supports such as photos
can help in preparing individuals with

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.159
autism for a new place for a visit
such as a stately home, a new person

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.789
in their life, like a support worker
or a new activity such as swimming.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.620
Making a book of photos, or a collection
of images on their device, can

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.490
give them an opportunity to look at
something before and during the change.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.470
BETH: If the change is because of a move
to a new school or care service, staff

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.460
that the individual will interact with,
should be a key part of the preparations.

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:49.774
They could be introduced to them
in familiar surroundings and

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.510
then be introduced to the new
environment by photos and a visit.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.080
JOE: Problems with sequencing can often
cause problems with behaviour when

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:02.600
transitioning between activities, for
example becoming anxious at school or

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.729
college over unstructured break times.

00:02:05.129 --> 00:02:08.760
Visual timetables either digital
or physical can help to alleviate

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.030
stress and timer alerts can inform
when things are over or due to start

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.890
making a timeframe more structured.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.380
BETH: Whatever the change, if the
individual can be prepared in some

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.879
way for what is about to happen, then
they have the time to process the

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.150
information and deal with it more
appropriately from an informed position.

